support.code:
const pause = () => Promise.resolve("ready");

async function load() {
  console.log("A: before await");
  const value = await pause();
  console.log("C:", value);
  return 42;
}

const result = load();
console.log("B: caller continues");
result.then(value => console.log("D:", value));

sections.content:
**Continuation model.** Execution runs synchronously until it reaches `await`. The async function records where to continue and gives control back. A later Promise job resumes it with either a value or a thrown reason.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant Caller
  participant AsyncFunction
  participant Promise
  Caller->>AsyncFunction: call()
  AsyncFunction->>Promise: await pending work
  AsyncFunction-->>Caller: return outer Promise
  Promise-->>AsyncFunction: settle and resume later
  AsyncFunction-->>Caller: fulfill or reject outer Promise
```

**Runnable example.** The caller continues after the async function reaches its first await.

```javascript
const pause = () => Promise.resolve("ready");

async function load() {
  console.log("inside before await");
  const value = await pause();
  console.log("inside after await", value);
  return 42;
}

const result = load();
console.log("caller continues");
console.log(await result); // 42
```

**Boundary.** A long loop before the first await still blocks the thread, and an await inside that loop can serialize work. Web Workers or worker threads are the relevant tool for CPU-bound parallel execution.

support.code:
const wait = (ms, value) => new Promise(resolve => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms);
});

async function loadDashboard() {
  const [user, notices] = await Promise.all([
    wait(30, "user"),
    wait(10, "notices"),
  ]);
  console.log(user, notices);
}

loadDashboard();

sections.content:
**Timing relationship.** Sequential code places the second start after the first settlement. Concurrent code creates both Promises first, allowing their underlying operations to overlap, then joins their outcomes.

| Requirement | Pattern | Failure result |
|---|---|---|
| B needs A | `await A(); await B(result)` | Stop or handle at dependency boundary |
| All independent results required | `await Promise.all([...])` | Combined Promise rejects |
| Every independent outcome required | `await Promise.allSettled([...])` | Records contain each status |

**Runnable example.** Both delays start before the join, so total time is near the slower delay rather than their sum.

```javascript
const delay = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms);
});

const started = Date.now();
const [slow, fast] = await Promise.all([
  delay(25, "slow"),
  delay(5, "fast"),
]);
console.log(slow, fast); // slow fast: input order
console.log(Date.now() - started < 45); // true on a normal run
```

**Boundary.** Concurrency can overload a server when thousands of tasks start at once. Real batch systems may need a concurrency limit rather than either one-at-a-time execution or unlimited `Promise.all`.

support.code:
const failLater = () => Promise.reject(new Error("save failed"));

async function saveSafely() {
  try {
    await failLater();
    return { ok: true };
  } catch (error) {
    return { ok: false, message: error.message };
  }
}

saveSafely().then(console.log);

sections.content:
**Ownership rule.** A Promise should have a visible owner: the current function awaits it, the function returns it to a caller, or an explicit detached-task handler records its failure. Losing all three paths creates silent timing bugs.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  P[Start async operation] --> Q{Who owns outcome?}
  Q -->|current function| A[await inside try/catch]
  Q -->|caller| R[return Promise]
  Q -->|intentional background task| H[attach explicit rejection handler]
  Q -->|nobody| U[unhandled rejection risk]
```

**Runnable example.** The inner catch handles the awaited failure and converts it into a useful result.

```javascript
const failLater = () => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("save failed")), 5);
});

async function saveSafely() {
  try {
    await failLater();
    return { ok: true };
  } catch (error) {
    return { ok: false, message: error.message };
  }
}

console.log(await saveSafely());
```

**Boundary.** `return await` is usually unnecessary for simple forwarding, but it is needed when a surrounding `try/catch/finally` must observe settlement before the async function exits that scope.
